     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><br><div>This html article : <a href=\"http://stla.github.io/stlapblog/posts/Ruby_is_cool.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://stla.github.io/stlapblog/posts/Ruby_is_cool.html&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792355016651000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1avhgsGbayYuvsKPVmNtO0\">http://stla.github.io/stlapblog/posts/Ruby_is_cool.html</a></div><div>is generated by this Rmd : <a href=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/stla/stlapblog/gh-pages/posts/Ruby_is_cool.Rmd\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://raw.githubusercontent.com/stla/stlapblog/gh-pages/posts/Ruby_is_cool.Rmd&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792355016651000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1izSN1ZJW31AqyN8E4_ck_\">https://raw.githubusercontent.com/stla/stlapblog/gh-pages/posts/Ruby_is_cool.Rmd</a></div><div><br></div><div>The Rmd file includes a RUBY engine. The problem with the ruby engine currently implemented in knitr returns all the block code and then all the outputs: it does not split according to each time the code generates an output. My small RUBY engine is not intended to replace the one in knitr: it is far to be perfect !</div><div><br></div><div>The idea is, starting from a block code in a chunk like :</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#008\">def</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> f</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">a</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  w</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">a</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">a<br>  </span><span style=\"color:#008\">return</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> w<br></span><span style=\"color:#008\">end</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>puts f</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\">y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#008\">def</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> g</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">a</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#008\">return</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> a</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">a</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">a<br></span><span style=\"color:#008\">end</span></div></code></div><div><br></div></div><div>Then the RUBY engine will firstly generates this code and then send it to Ruby :</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">puts </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&#39;&gt;&gt; x=2&#39;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>puts </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&#39;&gt;&gt; y=3<br>y=3<br>puts &#39;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;&gt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">def</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> f</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">a</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  w</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">a</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">a<br>  </span><span style=\"color:#008\">return</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> w<br></span><span style=\"color:#008\">end</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&#39;<br>def f(a)<br>  w=a*a<br>  return w<br>end<br>puts &#39;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;&gt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> puts f</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\">y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&#39;<br>puts f(x+y)<br>puts &#39;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;&gt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">def</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> g</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">a</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#008\">return</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> a</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">a</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">a<br></span><span style=\"color:#008\">end</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&#39;<br>def g(a)<br>  return a*a*a<br>end</span></div></code></div><div><br></div></div><div>Moreover there is an option <b>memo</b> to memorize the code in a chunk and an option <b>use.memo</b> to call the code in memory before running the current code. </div><div>It is far to be perfect but there many possible things to improve.</div></div>"
